{{templatestyles|Template:Pseudoheading/styles.css}}
{{-}}

== Pseudoheading ==
{{tl|Pseudoheading}} generates a classed div with an ID, as an alternative to HTML heading elements (which MediaWiki will often override the styles of); the ID can be used for anchor links. It ''explicitly'' has no support for direct CSS formatting, and applies no styles except for highlighting the text when a link to the anchor is followed. It is designed for use with classes and [[Help:Page styles|index-specific CSS]].

=== Parameters ===
* {{parameter|1}}: The content of the header.
* {{parameter|class}}: Optional CSS classes (use this if you have multiple heading styles in a single work). The div always has the class <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline="yes">.wst-heading</syntaxhighlight> as well as additional classes.
* {{parameter|id}}: Optional ID (used as an anchor). If not given, the contents of the header is used. This parameter should also be used to add a suitable simple ID, if markup or complex formatting is used in the header (such as a footnote directly associated with the heading). To omit the ID, set <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline="yes">id = </syntaxhighlight>.
* {{parameter|attr}}: Additional attributes.
*: For example, {{tl|pseudoheading/leveled}} uses this to set <syntaxhighlight lang="wikitext" inline="yes">attr = aria-level="{{{level|2}}}"</syntaxhighlight>.

=== Examples ===
{{doc example|
A div with class <code>wst-heading</code> and id <code>Chapter_One</code>:
<pre>{{pseudoheading|Chapter One}}</pre>
{{pseudoheading|Chapter One}}
}}

{{doc example|
A div with classes <code>.wst-heading</code> and <code>.chapter_heading</code> and id <code>Chapter_1</code>:
<pre>
{{pseudoheading|Chapter ''One''<ref>example</ref>|id=Chapter 1|class=chapter_heading}}
<references />
</pre>
{{pseudoheading|Chapter ''One''<ref>example</ref>|id=Chapter 1|class=chapter_heading}}
<references />
}}

There are a few common styling choices you may wish to replicate.

==== Margin/leading underneath the heading ====

Example: [[Page:Ayn Rand Anthem.pdf/21]] (using [[Index:Ayn Rand Anthem.pdf/styles.css]])

<syntaxhighlight lang=css>
.wst-heading {
	text-align:center;
	font-size:120%;
	margin-bottom:1.5em;
}
</syntaxhighlight>

==== Small-caps for subtitles ====

<syntaxhighlight lang=css>
.wst-heading {
	text-align:center;
}
.wst-heading.subtitle {
	font-variant:small-caps;
}
</syntaxhighlight>

Also consider <code>all-small-caps</code> if you want Proper Nouns to appear in "all small caps", including the first letters: e.g. {{asc|Oxford}}.

== Pseudoheading/inline ==
{{tl|Pseudoheading/inline}} has the same behavior as {{tl|pseudoheading}}, except that it generates a span rather than a div and adds the additional class <code>.wst-heading-inline</code>.

== Pseudoheading/main and Pseudoheading/subheading ==
{{tl|Pseudoheading/main}} and {{tl|pseudoheading/subheading}} call {{tl|pseudoheading}} with the additional classes <code>.wst-main-heading</code> and <code>.wst-subheading</code> respectively, and produce centered text, which {{tl|pseudoheading/main}} also makes font size 120%.

=== Parameters ===
* {{parameter|1}} The content of the header.
* {{parameter|class}} Optional CSS classes.
* {{parameter|id}} Optional ID.

=== Examples ===
{{doc example|
<pre>
{{pseudoheading/main|Chapter I}}
{{pseudoheading/subheading|Subtitle}}
</pre>
{{pseudoheading/main|Chapter I}}
{{pseudoheading/subheading|Subtitle}}
}}

== Pseudoheading/leveled ==
{{tl|Pseudoheading/leveled}} calls {{tl|pseudoheading}} with the additional classes <code>.wst-heading-leveled</code> and <code><nowiki>.wst-heading-leveled-{{{level}}}</nowiki></code>.

=== Parameters ===
* {{parameter|1}} The content of the header.
* {{parameter|class}} Optional CSS classes. The div always has the classes <code>.wst-heading</code>, <code>.wst-heading-leveled</code> and <code><nowiki>.wst-heading-leveled-{{{level}}}</nowiki></code> as well as additional classes.
* {{parameter|id}} Optional ID (used as an anchor).
* {{parameter|level}} Heading level. Typically an integer between 1 and 6, defaults to 2.

=== Examples ===
{{doc example|
<pre>
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Text}}
</pre>
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Text}}
}}

{{doc example|
<pre>
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 1|level=1}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 2|level=2}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 3|level=3}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 4|level=4}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 5|level=5}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 6|level=6}}
</pre>
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 1|level=1}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 2|level=2}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 3|level=3}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 4|level=4}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 5|level=5}}
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Level 6|level=6}}
}}


{{doc example|
<pre>
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Text<ref>Demo</ref>|class=example|id=Text|level=example}}
<references />
</pre>
{{Pseudoheading/leveled|Text<ref>Demo</ref>|class=example|id=Text|level=example}}
<references />
}}

